-- Doc 03 §7.6's disclosed trap, rejected rather than documented away.
--
-- The tail of a draw arrow becomes a **closure**, so this `return` leaves the
-- closure and not `run` — and the result is valid Rust in which
-- `with_connection` receives the `Err` the author meant the function to
-- return. Nothing in the source hints that the block became a closure, and
-- the author's model (`return` returns from the function) is correct
-- everywhere else in the language.
--
-- `?` is unaffected and must stay so: it propagates out of the closure, which
-- is what the construct exists for.
run :: Result<(), Error>
run =
  conn <- pool.with_connection
  return Err Error::Bad
  Ok ()
